    <h2 align="center">Experiment 2 : Solar Panel</h2>
    <div class="container-fluid">
      <h4> Concept </h4>
      <p>Photovoltaic (PV) cells are semiconductors which become electrically conductive on exposure to light or heat.</p>
      <p>
        <figure class="figure" align="center">
          <img alt="Fig1" src="_images\fig1.jpg" />
          <figcaption class="figure-caption"><small>Figure 1: A buck converter on a printed circuit board</small></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </p>
      <p>
        Open-circuit voltage The open circuit voltage is dependent on the irradiance. Figure 2 shows the relationship of open circuit
        voltage with irradiance. The open circuit voltage is a nonlinear function of the irradiance. The voltage approaches
        its maximum level even at low irradiance. That is way solar panels are able to produce substantial voltages even
        in cloudy weathers. Short-circuit current Short circuit current is the largest possible current a PV cell can generate.
        The current is measured by connecting an ammeter with extremely low internal resistance directly to the PV cell.
        Note PV cells are short circuit proof. They will not be damaged if the output terminals are short circuited.
      </p>
      <p>
        <figure class="figure" align=center>
          <img class="img-responsive" alt="Fig2" src="_images\fig2.jpg" />
          <figcaption class="figure-caption"><small>Figure 2. Schematics of a buck converter</small></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </p>
      <p>
        <figure class="figure" align=center>
          <img class="img-responsive" alt="Fig3" src="_images\fig3.jpg" />
          <figcaption class="figure-caption"><small>Figure 3. Equivalent circuit when the switch is closed</small></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </p>
      <p>
        <figure class="figure" align="center">
          <img class="img-responsive" alt="Fig4" src="_images\fig4.jpg" />
          <figcaption class="figure-caption"><small>Figure 4. Equivalent circuit when the switch is open</small></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </p>
      <p>
        <figure class="figure" align=center>
          <img class="img-responsive" alt="Fig5" src="_images\fig5.jpg" />
        </figure>
      </p>
      <h4> Learning Objective </h4>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>Measuring open circuit voltage</li>
          <li>Measuring short circuit current</li>
          <li>Recording solar panel characteristics</li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <h4> Materials and Equipment </h4>
      <p>Solar panel, potentiomer, multi-meter</p>

      <h4>Procedure</h4>
      <div>
        <ol>
          <li>Measuring open-circuit voltage Connect a voltmeter directly to the solar panel to measure the open circuit
            voltage. See Figure 1. Vary the irradiance from xxx to xxx and record the open-circuit voltage in a graph</li>
          <li>Measuring short-circuit current Connect an ammeter directly to the solar panel to measure the short circuit
            current. See Figure 3.</li>
          <li>Recording characteristics of a solar panel:
            <o1>
              <li>Set the irradiance constant.</li>
              <li>Connect a potentiometer to the solar panel.</li>
              <li>Vary the potentiometer from 0 to 10 kΩ</li>
              <li>Measure the output voltage and current</li>
              <li>Record the voltage and current in a graph</li>
            </o1>
          </li>
          <li>From the graph, identify the maximum power point. What is the output voltage and current at the maximum power
            point?
          </li>
          <li>Calculate the fill factor.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
